-- A failing `assert` inside a handler ends *that frame*, not the program:
-- the handler's answer becomes an `Exception` and the caller carries on
-- (phase 4, 2026-08-28, see docs/todo/errors-as-particles.md).
--
-- The rule, in the owner's words: "assert fail olması demek şu anlama
-- gelmeli — return Exception". At the top level there is no frame to return
-- into, so a failing assert there still ends the program with a non-zero
-- status; `fail_assert_basic.code` and friends pin that half.
Check { n } => {
    assert n = 1
    return Ok { }
}

emit Check { "n": 2 } to this get bad
assert bad is Exception

-- The whole particle, field by field, because the two backends build this
-- independently (interpreter.rs's `exception`, runtime.c's
-- `code_take_failure`) and the harness only compares pass/fail — a shape
-- that drifted between them would otherwise go unnoticed.
assert bad = {
    "_class": "Exception",
    "source": "core",
    "message": "assertion failed",
    "innerException": null
}

-- The same handler still works afterwards: a failed frame leaves nothing
-- behind, including the re-entry guard its exit block clears.
emit Check { "n": 1 } to this get good
assert good is Ok
